#include <list>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>

template <typename T>
class FeedbackProcessor {
 public:
  // ... (Implement functions for processing feedback, handling attachments, etc.)

  void insertAfterFirstOccurrence(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& target, 
                                  const std::string& insertion) {
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
    auto it = list.begin();
    while (it != list.end() && *it != target) {
      ++it;
    }
    if (it != list.end()) {
      list.insert_after(it, insertion);
    }
  }

 private:
  std::mutex mutex_; 
};

``` 



**Time and Space Complexity Analysis:**

* **Time Complexity:** O(n) in the worst case where `target` is not found, iterating through all elements of the list. In the best case (O(1)) if `target` is found at the beginning of the list.
* **Space Complexity:** O(1) - The function uses a constant amount of additional space regardless of the input size.

**Edge Cases and Potential Bottlenecks:**

* **Concurrent Modifications:** The `std::mutex` ensures thread safety, preventing race conditions during concurrent insertions. 
* **Large Datasets:** For extremely large lists, consider using more efficient data structures like `std::vector` or implementing a parallel insertion algorithm for further performance optimization.


